WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.750
Film Courage: Have you ever thought of stopping?

00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:05.610
Have you said to yourself ‘There are easier
ways to spend my life.’

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:06.920
Kelli McNeil, Writer, Actress and Voice Over
Artist: All the time.

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:14.480
I mean I’ve carved out a career for myself
as a marketing and creative professional,

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.300
copywriting and art directing (things like
that).

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:22.619
If I really gave myself to that maybe I could
be very successful but I feel like there would

00:00:22.619 --> 00:00:27.570
be this hole inside of me that would just
be devoid.

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:30.240
I think that maybe I’m not very smart.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.380
Maybe I should have given up a long time ago
because I keep putting myself through this

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:39.060
and trying and keep going but I think that’s
part of the challenge that a lot of writers

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:44.440
face is the self-doubt and the imposter syndrome
and especially as a woman and trying to break

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:47.100
through in this industry it’s very hard.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:53.110
Yeah…you grapple with that all the time
and writing by nature is a very solitary activity.

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:59.550
It’s very easy to want to throw in the towel
and that’s why it’s so important to celebrate

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:07.940
those little victories, just one little thing,
one connection, one interview, one chance

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:11.360
encounter could change your world.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:16.440
Film Courage: But by naming the imposter syndrome
and sort of calling it out, does that take

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:17.900
away its power a little bit?

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:20.660
Kelli: You have to be aware of it when it’s
happening.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:25.110
There are times and I think this comes with
age and maturity as well.

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:30.080
Certainly I am more grounded now in my 30’s
then I was in my 20’s.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:33.500
I’m able to own my mistakes more readily.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:46.930
I’m able to objectively look at myself and
separate the part of me that needs approval

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:51.490
to the part of me that is trying to create
something as well.

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:58.840
If I can give myself enough space to fail,
then I can help overcome that imposter syndrome

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.060
but it’s not an easy thing to do.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:03.040
I know a lot of people grapple with it.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:07.800
Film Courage: When you’re in that space
of writing about some character that is so

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:12.970
real, it’s speaking to you, it’s kind
of scary, it might be a little edgy, that

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:15.560
sounds like it’s not being an imposter by
any means?

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:20.760
Kelli: That’s such a rare state of enlightenment
that…like I said it’s almost like getting

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:21.930
a runner’s high.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:24.900
It doesn’t happen all the time but it is
magic when it does.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:25.360
Question: How many times have you quit writing?

